，PD-L2 通过与 T 细胞表面的 RGMb 结合发挥抑制作用，导致 CD8+ T 细胞活性受抑 [P0242]。特定细菌物种（如*Coprobacillus cateniformis*）的定植可通过表面提取物经 MYD88 依赖途径下调 DC 上的 PD-L2 表达，从而恢复对抗 PD-L1 治疗的敏感性 [P0242]。在 T 细胞特异性敲除 RGMb 的小鼠中，抗 PD-L1 疗效得到改善，而在巨噬细胞中敲除则无效，表明 RGMb 在 T 细胞上的抑制性信号是关键机制 [P0242]。这一发现揭示了微生物调节免疫检查点的新维度：通过改变检查点配体的表达水平，微生物群能设定免疫反应的阈值，这解释了为何部分患者对 PD-1/PD-L1 治疗无响应，而联合阻断 PD-L2/RGMb 通路可克服这种耐药。\\n\\n## 屏障完整性与髓系环境重塑\\n\\n微生物信号到达肿瘤的前提是跨越肠道屏障，而屏障状态本身受微生物群调节并反过来影响免疫后果。利用集成蠕动功能的人源化肠-on-a-chip 模型研究发现，非响应者（NR）的粪便样本会导致肠道上皮屏障完整性显著受损（ZO-1 和β-catenin 下调），并引发强烈的促炎反应（如 IL-6、TNF 信号激活）[P0073]。相比之下，响应者粪便中的可溶性因子（富含鞭毛蛋白相关基因）能激活 TLR-5 信号并促进 IL-1β释放，有助于维持屏障稳态 [P0073]。这种屏障功能的差异直接关联到细菌易位的风险与性质。\\n\\n细菌易位并非单纯的病理过程，其在特定情境下可介导治疗获益，但也伴随毒性风险。*Paraclostridium bifermentans*的研究揭示了这种双重角色：该菌在急性免疫性结肠炎患者中富集，其活菌能破坏上皮屏障完整性并加剧结肠炎（irAE）；然而，其分泌的小分子代谢物（< 3 kDa）却能增强抗肿瘤免疫反应 [P0284]。在动物模型中，口服*P. bifermentans*增强了对双 ICI 的抗肿瘤响应，且这种有益效应由无细胞上清液保留，而屏障破坏效应仅由活菌引起 [P0284]。这表明，同一微生物可通过不同组分（活菌体 vs 代谢物）分别驱动毒性和疗效，且屏障破坏可能是炎症性毒性（如结肠炎）的结构基础，而代谢物则是系统性免疫增强的介质。\\n\\n髓系环境在屏障受损后的免疫后果中起核心作用。在胰腺导管腺癌（PDAC）模型中，肠道菌群通过细菌易位影响肿瘤内微生物组成，进而调节肿瘤相关巨噬细胞（TAMs）的极化 [P0084]。暴露于 PDAC 患者肠道菌群的巨噬细胞表现出抑制 T 细胞活化的能力，并通过 TLR 信号通路诱导免疫耐受；清除肠道菌群后，TAMs 向促炎的 M1 样表型转化，增强 T 细胞激活 [P0084]。在结直肠癌中，具核梭杆菌（*F. nucleatum*）通过抑制 cGAS-IFN-β通路减少 CD8+ T 细胞浸润，而*B. fragilis*多糖可与 CTLA-4 抑制剂协同增强 T 细胞活化 [P0028]。这些机制共同说明，屏障状态、细菌易位与髓系重塑形成连续过程：屏障完整性决定信号通路的性质（稳态 vs 炎症），到达肿瘤后的微生物成分进一步通过髓系细胞调节 T 细胞的浸润与功能。\\n\\n表 2-1 总结了不同实体瘤模型中已验证的关键免疫学机制及其依赖的分子与细胞要素。这些机制在不同癌种中表现出显著的异质性，例如 PD-L2/RGMb 轴主要在 MC38 结肠癌和 B16 黑色素瘤模型中验证，而 SFB 诱导的 T_H17 可塑性则在多种表达相应抗原的肿瘤模型中观察到。这种异质性进一步强调了在解释微生物 - 免疫互作时，必须结合具体的肿瘤类型、免疫微环境背景及微生物组分进行综合分析，而非寻求单一的通用机制。\\n\\n| 机制类别 | 关键分子/通路 | 主要免疫细胞 | 肿瘤模型/癌种 | 效应方向 | 来源证据 |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **先天识别** | LPS 酰化状态 (六酰化 vs 五酰化) | 巨噬细胞、DC | MC38 结肠癌、黑色素瘤 | 六酰化增强疗效，五酰化拮抗 | [P0080] |\\n| **先天识别** | MEP 通路 (HMBPP) / 核黄素通路 (5-OP-RU) | Vδ2 T 细胞 / MAIT 细胞 | MMR-d/MSI-H 实体瘤 | MEP 增强杀伤，核黄素抑制 CD4+ | [P0444] |\\n| **抗原呈递** | TLRs-IRF8 轴 | CD103+ DC、CD8+ T 细胞 | 实体瘤 (Ruminococcaceae 定植) | 促进 DC 迁移与 T 细胞激活 | [P0114] |\\n| **空间转运** | CCR7 依赖性细菌转运 | DC、MLN、TDLN | B16 黑色素瘤 | 肠道细菌转移至肿瘤增强疗效 | [P0166] |\\n| **分子模拟** | TCR 交叉反应 (SFB 抗原) | T_H17 -> ex-T_H17、CD8+ T 细胞 | B16、LLC1、MC-38 | 增强 ICB 疗效，诱导记忆保护 | [P0388] |\\n| **分子模拟** | 自身抗原 vs 肿瘤抗原模拟 | T 细胞 | NSCLC | 肿瘤抗原模拟增强疗效，自身抗原模拟驱动 irAE | [P0462] |\\n| **检查点调节** | PD-L2 / RGMb 轴 | DC、T 细胞 | MC38、B16 | 微生物下调 PD-L2 克服耐药 | [P0242] |\\n| **屏障与易位** | 紧密连接蛋白 (ZO-1)、TLR5 | 上皮细胞、内皮细胞 | 黑色素瘤 (肠芯片模型) | 响应者维持屏障，非响应者破坏 | [P0073] |\\n| **屏障与易位** | 活菌体 vs 小分子代谢物 (<3 kDa) | 上皮细胞、T 细胞 | 黑色素瘤、NSCLC | 活菌致炎 (irAE)，代谢物增效 | [P0284] |\\n| **髓系重塑** | TLR 信号、cGAS-IFN-β | TAMs、MDSCs | PDAC、CRC | 菌群耗竭促进 M1 极化，Fn 抑制 T 细胞 | [P0084], [P0028] |\\n\\n上述机制共同描绘了微生物调节 ICI 疗效的免疫学图景：从肠道内的分子结构识别开始，经由 DC 介导的空间转运与抗原呈递，通过 T 细胞交叉反应放大特异性，最终在肿瘤微环境中通过检查点调节和髓系重塑决定治疗结局。这一过程受到屏障完整性的严格约束，且不同组分（如活菌与代谢物）可能分别介导疗效与毒性。这些免疫过程的具体执行，很大程度上依赖于可传播的化学信号，如代谢物和细胞因子，这构成了下一章讨论的核心内容。\\n\\n```guide_writer_metadata\\n{\\\"complete\\\":true,\\\"remaining_content\\\":[]}\\n```\",\"reasoning_content\":\"The user wants me to write Chapter 2 of the manuscript based on the provided materials and guidelines.\\nChapter 2 is titled \\\"微生物调节 ICI 疗效的免疫学机制\\\" (Immunological Mechanisms of Microbiome Modulation of ICI Efficacy).\\n\\nKey requirements from `chapter_assignment`:\\n1.  **Focus**: Explain the immunological mechanisms as a followable process.\\n2.  **Content**:\\n    *   How microbial signals are recognized.\\n    *   How they affect antigen presentation and T cells.\\n    *   Why spatial transport and myeloid environment change outcomes.\\n    *   Innate recognition tasks around \\\"why same microbe doesn't always mean same immune response\\\" (molecular structure, receptors, DC subsets, checkpoint regulation).\\n    *   Adaptive response: molecular mimicry, cross-reactivity, priming, differentiation, exhaustion.\\n    *   Barrier, translocation, myeloid remodeling as a continuous discussion.\\n    *   Include important experimental controls and model boundaries.\\n    *   Include a table comparing mechanisms across different solid tumors (as per `required_content`).\\n3.  **Style**:\\n    *   Do not restart as a mini-review; continue from Chapter 1's established understanding (clinical signals need mechanistic explanation).\\n    *   Do not list terms; explain relationships.\\n    *   Do not add generic limitations or future outlooks at the end of every paragraph.\\n    *   Use specific objects and actions.\\n    *   Do not include guide instructions or internal IDs in the text.\\n    *   Use provided source materials (P0080, P0309, P0028, P0114, P0242, P0583, P0535, P0132, P0333, P0388, P0560, P0085, P0462, P0269, P0098, P0154, P0195, P0444, P0073, P0284, P0166, P0084, P0438, P0362, P0573, P0146, P0057, P0090, P0113, P0270, P0352, P0457).\\n    *   Output natural Markdown body text.\\n    *   Append metadata at the end.\\n\\nPlan:\\n1.  **Introduction**: Transition from Chapter 1's clinical associations to the need for mechanistic understanding. Why does the same microbe not always yield the same result? (Structure vs. Taxonomy).\\n2.  **Innate Recognition**: Focus on LPS structure (P0080) and PRR signaling (TLR, NOD2, STING). Explain how molecular structure dictates the signal (agonist vs. antagonist).\\n3.  **Antigen Presentation & T Cell Priming**: DC subsets (CD103+), migration from gut to tumor/dLNs (P0114, P0166). Molecular mimicry and cross-reactivity (P0085, P0388, P0462).\\n4.  **Adaptive Response & Checkpoints**: T cell differentiation, exhaustion, and the specific role of PD-1/PD-L1/PD-L2/RGMb axis (P0242).\\n5.  **Barrier & Translocation**: Gut barrier integrity, bacterial translocation to MLNs and tumors (P0073, P0166, P0284). Myeloid remodeling (TAMs, MDSCs).\\n6.  **Table**: Create a table summarizing mechanisms across different tumor types/models as required.\\n7.  **Conclusion**: Summarize how these processes are regulated by transmissible chemical signals, leading into Chapter 3 (metabolites).\\n\\nDrafting - Section by Section:\\n*   **Intro**: Chapter 1 established that taxonomy alone is insufficient. Need to look at function/structure.\\n*   **Innate**: P0080 is key here. Hexa-acylated vs. Penta-acylated LPS. TLR4 signaling. This explains why \\\"LPS-producing bacteria\\\" isn't a uniform bad/good label.\\n*   **DC & Migration**: P0114 (Ruminococcaceae -> CD103+ DCs -> CD8+ T cells). P0166 (ICT induces translocation to MLNs/Tumor via DCs/CCR7). This connects gut to tumor.\\n*   **Mimicry**: P0388 (SFB -> Th17 -> ex-Th17 in tumor). P0085 (Neoantigen mimicry). P0462 (Molecular mimicry driving irAEs vs efficacy).\\n*   **Checkpoints**: P0242 (PD-L2/RGMb axis). Microbiome downregulates PD-L2 on DCs.\\n*   **Barrier/Translocation**: P0073 (Gut-on-chip, barrier integrity differs in responders/non-responders). P0284 (P. bifermentans disrupts barrier but metabolite helps efficacy - dual role).\\n*   **Table**: Compare mechanisms (LPS, DC migration, Mimicry, Checkpoint modulation) across models (Melanoma, NSCLC, CRC, etc.).\\n\\nRefining the flow:\\nStart with the \\\"structure over taxonomy\\\" concept (P0080). Then move to how this signal is picked up by innate cells (DCs) and transported (P0114, P0166). Then discuss how T cells recognize this (Mimicry, P0388, P0085). Then discuss the checkpoint environment they encounter (P0242). Finally, discuss the physical route (Barrier/Translocation, P0073, P0284).\\n\\nEnsure I don't just list studies but weave them into the mechanism explanation.\\nEnsure I include the table.\\nEnsure I don't repeat Chapter 1's clinical trial details (like FMT-LUMINate ORR numbers) unless relevant to mechanism (e.g. FMT transferring the mechanism).\\n\\nLet's check the materials again for specific mechanism details.\\nP0080: Hexa-acylated LPS (lpxM) vs Penta-acylated (lpxL). TLR4 dependent. MC38 model.\\nP0114: Ruminococcaceae -> CD103+ DCs -> CD8+ T cells. IRF8 dependent.\\nP0166: ICT induces translocation of gut bacteria to MLNs/Tumor via DCs/CCR7. MLN resection abrogates effect.\\nP0388: SFB -> Th17 -> ex-Th17 in tumor. Molecular mimicry.\\nP0242: Microbiome downregulates PD-L2 on DCs. PD-L2 binds RGMb on T cells (inhibitory).\\nP0073: Gut-on-chip. Non-responder feces -> barrier disruption (ZO-1 down). Responder feces -> TLR5 activation, IL-1beta/IL-10.\\nP0284: P. bifermentans. Live bacteria disrupt barrier (irAE), metabolite (<3kDa) enhances efficacy.\\nP0462: Molecular mimicry (SLE epitopes -> irAEs; Lung epitopes -> Efficacy). Strain level variation.\\n\\nStructure:\\n1.  **From Taxonomy to Molecular Structure**: Why species names fail (P0080).\\n2.  **Innate Sensing and Antigen Presentation**: DC activation, migration (P0114, P0166).\\n3.  **T Cell Priming and Cross-Reactivity**: Mimicry, Th17 plasticity (P0388, P0085, P0462).\\n4.  **Checkpoint Modulation in the Microenvironment**: PD-L2/RGMb (P0242).\\n5.  **Barrier Integrity and Translocation**: The physical route and its dual nature (P0073, P0284).\\n6.  **Table**: Mechanism comparison.\\n\\nWriting in Chinese.\\nAvoid \\\"Chapter 2\\\" header in the text, just start the content. But the prompt asks for \\\"Chapter 2 ... new body text\\\". I should probably include the Chapter Title as per standard manuscript format, but the guide says \\\"Title serves content... no uniform sentence pattern\\\". I will use the assigned title.\\nWait, `accepted_body_markdown` ends with Chapter 1. I need to continue. I will start with the Chapter 2 Title.\\n\\nCheck constraints: \\\"Do not output thought process or self-score.\\\" \\\"Output natural Markdown body text.\\\" \\\"Append metadata.\\\"\\n\\nLet's draft.\\nStart with the limitation of taxonomy established in Ch1.\\nMove to P0080 (LPS structure).\\nMove to P0114/P0166 (DC migration).\\nMove to P0388/P0085 (T cell mimicry).\\nMove to P0242 (Checkpoint modulation).\\nMove to P0073/P0284 (Barrier/Translocation).\\nInsert Table.\\nConclude leading to metabolites (Ch3).\\n\\nMake sure to cite sources like [P0080].\\nEnsure the table is meaningful (Mechanism, Key Molecules/Cells, Tumor Model, Outcome).\\n\\nOne detail from P0270: Akkermansia negative in prostate cancer. This supports the \\\"context dependency\\\" theme.\\nOne detail from P0444: Functional pathways (MEP, Riboflavin) more stable than taxonomy. This supports the \\\"function over taxonomy\\\" theme.\\n\\nOkay, ready to write.\"}}],\"id\":\"chatcmpl-689c6524-384a-9b5f-acdd-38db8e5aba99\",\"model\":\"qwen3.5-plus\",\"usage\":{\"completion_tokens\":5563,\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":2185,\"text_tokens\":5563},\"prompt_tokens\":219888,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"text_tokens\":219888},\"total_tokens\":225451}}",
  "raw_stream_sha256": "d738158c52d1ee79af7abfbb8a70a4b5ef4efc4aa392205f97023668df256a86",
  "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to write Chapter 2 of the manuscript based on the provided materials and guidelines.\nChapter 2 is titled \"微生物调节 ICI 疗效的免疫学机制\" (Immunological Mechanisms of Microbiome Modulation of ICI Efficacy).\n\nKey requirements from `chapter_assignment`:\n1.  **Focus**: Explain the immunological mechanisms as a followable process.\n2.  **Content**:\n    *   How microbial signals are recognized.\n    *   How they affect antigen presentation and T cells.\n    *   Why spatial transport and myeloid environment change outcomes.\n    *   Innate recognition tasks around \"why same microbe doesn't always mean same immune response\" (molecular structure, receptors, DC subsets, checkpoint regulation).\n    *   Adaptive response: molecular mimicry, cross-reactivity, priming, differentiation, exhaustion.\n    *   Barrier, translocation, myeloid remodeling as a continuous discussion.\n    *   Include important experimental controls and model boundaries.\n    *   Include a table comparing mechanisms across different solid tumors (as per `required_content`).\n3.  **Style**:\n    *   Do not restart as a mini-review; continue from Chapter 1's established understanding (clinical signals need mechanistic explanation).\n    *   Do not list terms; explain relationships.\n    *   Do not add generic limitations or future outlooks at the end of every paragraph.\n    *   Use specific objects and actions.\n    *   Do not include guide instructions or internal IDs in the text.\n    *   Use provided source materials (P0080, P0309, P0028, P0114, P0242, P0583, P0535, P0132, P0333, P0388, P0560, P0085, P0462, P0269, P0098, P0154, P0195, P0444, P0073, P0284, P0166, P0084, P0438, P0362, P0573, P0146, P0057, P0090, P0113, P0270, P0352, P0457).\n    *   Output natural Markdown body text.\n    *   Append metadata at the end.\n\nPlan:\n1.  **Introduction**: Transition from Chapter 1's clinical associations to the need for mechanistic understanding. Why does the same microbe not always yield the same result? (Structure vs. Taxonomy).\n2.  **Innate Recognition**: Focus on LPS structure (P0080) and PRR signaling (TLR, NOD2, STING). Explain how molecular structure dictates the signal (agonist vs. antagonist).\n3.  **Antigen Presentation & T Cell Priming**: DC subsets (CD103+), migration from gut to tumor/dLNs (P0114, P0166). Molecular mimicry and cross-reactivity (P0085, P0388, P0462).\n4.  **Adaptive Response & Checkpoints**: T cell differentiation, exhaustion, and the specific role of PD-1/PD-L1/PD-L2/RGMb axis (P0242).\n5.  **Barrier & Translocation**: Gut barrier integrity, bacterial translocation to MLNs and tumors (P0073, P0166, P0284). Myeloid remodeling (TAMs, MDSCs).\n6.  **Table**: Create a table summarizing mechanisms across different tumor types/models as required.\n7.  **Conclusion**: Summarize how these processes are regulated by transmissible chemical signals, leading into Chapter 3 (metabolites).\n\nDrafting - Section by Section:\n*   **Intro**: Chapter 1 established that taxonomy alone is insufficient. Need to look at function/structure.\n*   **Innate**: P0080 is key here. Hexa-acylated vs. Penta-acylated LPS. TLR4 signaling. This explains why \"LPS-producing bacteria\" isn't a uniform bad/good label.\n*   **DC & Migration**: P0114 (Ruminococcaceae -> CD103+ DCs -> CD8+ T cells). P0166 (ICT induces translocation to MLNs/Tumor via DCs/CCR7). This connects gut to tumor.\n*   **Mimicry**: P0388 (SFB -> Th17 -> ex-Th17 in tumor). P0085 (Neoantigen mimicry). P0462 (Molecular mimicry driving irAEs vs efficacy).\n*   **Checkpoints**: P0242 (PD-L2/RGMb axis). Microbiome downregulates PD-L2 on DCs.\n*   **Barrier/Translocation**: P0073 (Gut-on-chip, barrier integrity differs in responders/non-responders). P0284 (P. bifermentans disrupts barrier but metabolite helps efficacy - dual role).\n*   **Table**: Compare mechanisms (LPS, DC migration, Mimicry, Checkpoint modulation) across models (Melanoma, NSCLC, CRC, etc.).\n\nRefining the flow:\nStart with the \"structure over taxonomy\" concept (P0080). Then move to how this signal is picked up by innate cells (DCs) and transported (P0114, P0166). Then discuss how T cells recognize this (Mimicry, P0388, P0085). Then discuss the checkpoint environment they encounter (P0242). Finally, discuss the physical route (Barrier/Translocation, P0073, P0284).\n\nEnsure I don't just list studies but weave them into the mechanism explanation.\nEnsure I include the table.\nEnsure I don't repeat Chapter 1's clinical trial details (like FMT-LUMINate ORR numbers) unless relevant to mechanism (e.g. FMT transferring the mechanism).\n\nLet's check the materials again for specific mechanism details.\nP0080: Hexa-acylated LPS (lpxM) vs Penta-acylated (lpxL). TLR4 dependent. MC38 model.\nP0114: Ruminococcaceae -> CD103+ DCs -> CD8+ T cells. IRF8 dependent.\nP0166: ICT induces translocation of gut bacteria to MLNs/Tumor via DCs/CCR7. MLN resection abrogates effect.\nP0388: SFB -> Th17 -> ex-Th17 in tumor. Molecular mimicry.\nP0242: Microbiome downregulates PD-L2 on DCs. PD-L2 binds RGMb on T cells (inhibitory).\nP0073: Gut-on-chip. Non-responder feces -> barrier disruption (ZO-1 down). Responder feces -> TLR5 activation, IL-1beta/IL-10.\nP0284: P. bifermentans. Live bacteria disrupt barrier (irAE), metabolite (<3kDa) enhances efficacy.\nP0462: Molecular mimicry (SLE epitopes -> irAEs; Lung epitopes -> Efficacy). Strain level variation.\n\nStructure:\n1.  **From Taxonomy to Molecular Structure**: Why species names fail (P0080).\n2.  **Innate Sensing and Antigen Presentation**: DC activation, migration (P0114, P0166).\n3.  **T Cell Priming and Cross-Reactivity**: Mimicry, Th17 plasticity (P0388, P0085, P0462).\n4.  **Checkpoint Modulation in the Microenvironment**: PD-L2/RGMb (P0242).\n5.  **Barrier Integrity and Translocation**: The physical route and its dual nature (P0073, P0284).\n6.  **Table**: Mechanism comparison.\n\nWriting in Chinese.\nAvoid \"Chapter 2\" header in the text, just start the content. But the prompt asks for \"Chapter 2 ... new body text\". I should probably include the Chapter Title as per standard manuscript format, but the guide says \"Title serves content... no uniform sentence pattern\". I will use the assigned title.\nWait, `accepted_body_markdown` ends with Chapter 1. I need to continue. I will start with the Chapter 2 Title.\n\nCheck constraints: \"Do not output thought process or self-score.\" \"Output natural Markdown body text.\" \"Append metadata.\"\n\nLet's draft.\nStart with the limitation of taxonomy established in Ch1.\nMove to P0080 (LPS structure).\nMove to P0114/P0166 (DC migration).\nMove to P0388/P0085 (T cell mimicry).\nMove to P0242 (Checkpoint modulation).\nMove to P0073/P0284 (Barrier/Translocation).\nInsert Table.\nConclude leading to metabolites (Ch3).\n\nMake sure to cite sources like [P0080].\nEnsure the table is meaningful (Mechanism, Key Molecules/Cells, Tumor Model, Outcome).\n\nOne detail from P0270: Akkermansia negative in prostate cancer. This supports the \"context dependency\" theme.\nOne detail from P0444: Functional pathways (MEP, Riboflavin) more stable than taxonomy. This supports the \"function over taxonomy\" theme.\n\nOkay, ready to write.",
